From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ggbdpq <ggbdpq@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 22:19:04 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] provider, plugin: a sign-in whose refresh token rotates is
 never carried (#5583)

A plugin whose vendor spends a refresh token once (the auth hook's
rotating) signs one computer in. Carried by a backup or a sync, the
second computer becomes the token's second owner: both renew it as it
comes due, the vendor turns the loser away (invalid_grant), and with no
newer generation anywhere in the sync path only a device sign-in brings
the account back (#5583, skalitz-wolf).

The auth hook declares rotating beside keepAlive and refreshLead; the
host passes it in the providers' row, and CollectSignIns leaves such
sign-ins out of what a backup or a sync carries, MergeSignIns and
RestoreSignIns leave out what an older backup or sync already carried.
Each computer signs in to such a provider itself; the local sign-ins
are untouched, and a copy already on the server falls out of the
carried set by the usual removal, on the next sync of each computer.

Tests: TestCollectSignInsLeavesARotatingSignInOut,
TestMergeSignInsDoesNotTakeARotatingSignInIn,
TestRestoreSignInsLeavesARotatingSignInOut (red before, green after;
go test ./internal/provider/ ./internal/backup/ ./internal/davsync/
-tags nogui; go vet ./..., GOOS=linux go vet -tags nogui ./...,
GOOS=windows go vet ./...; go build, GOOS=linux go build -tags nogui,
GOOS=windows go build).

